---
import Divider from "../../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";

//#region CodeSnippets
const Eight = `EIGHT PLACES THE SHEET IS NOT JAVASCRIPT

  1  lists start at index 1, not 0
  2  aList ← bList COPIES the list
  3  an out-of-bounds index STOPS the program
  4  REPEAT UNTIL is the inverse of while
  5  no indexed for-loop; keep the counter by hand
  6  no ELSE IF — nest IF inside ELSE
  7  RANDOM(a, b) includes both a and b
  8  the robot: direction is relative; illegal move
     stops the program`;

const DrillA_js = `let names = ["Ana", "Ben", "Cy"];

for (let i = 0; i < names.length; i++)
{
  console.log(names[i]);
}`;

const DrillA_ref = `names ← ["Ana", "Ben", "Cy"]
i ← 1

REPEAT LENGTH(names) TIMES
{
  DISPLAY(names[i])
  i ← i + 1
}`;

const DrillB = `x ← 10

REPEAT UNTIL(x < 3)
{
  DISPLAY(x)
  x ← x - 2
}`;

const DrillC = `aList ← [1, 2, 3]
bList ← aList
bList[1] ← 99

DISPLAY(aList[1])`;

const Robot = `The robot faces RIGHT. It runs:

  MOVE_FORWARD()
  ROTATE_LEFT()
  MOVE_FORWARD()

ROTATE_LEFT turns it 90° counter-clockwise.
Which way is it facing at the end, and how many
cells has it moved from the start?`;
//#endregion
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a href="/courses/ap-csp/" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3">
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back
        </a>

        <h1>Activity 4.5: The Reference Sheet Under Time</h1>
        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-5" />

        <!-- Brief -->
        <section id="brief">
          <h2>Eight Points, Every One Worth a Question</h2>

          <p>
            <b>The multiple-choice section is written in the exam's own notation, not
            in JavaScript.</b> You have translated between the two all year. Under
            time, in May, the eight places they differ are where a fluent reader
            slows down and a careless one loses marks.
          </p>

          <p>
            <b class="text-sn-theme">Barron's teaches you the notation. It does not
            know which eight trip you.</b> This course does &mdash; you have met each of
            them in a specific session &mdash; and today is drilling the switch until it
            is fast.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- The eight -->
        <section id="eight">
          <h2>The Eight, on One Card</h2>

          <Code code={Eight} frame="code" lang="txt" title="Carry This" showLineNumbers={false} class="mb-4" />

          <p>
            <b>Ordered by what they cost.</b> The first three change the answer
            silently &mdash; the code looks fine and returns something wrong or stops
            dead. The rest change the shape of the code you write or read.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Drills -->
        <section id="drills">
          <h2>The Drills</h2>

          <p>
            <b>Time yourself. Two minutes each.</b> That is roughly the pace a
            code question demands on the real paper.
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Drill 1: Translate to Notation</h3>
          <p>Rewrite this JavaScript in reference-sheet notation.</p>
          <Code code={DrillA_js} frame="code" lang="javascript" title="JavaScript" class="mt-3" />
          <p class="mt-3">
            <b>Two mismatches are waiting here:</b> there is no indexed{" "}
            <code>for</code> loop, and the first element is index <b>1</b>. A correct
            answer keeps its own counter:
          </p>
          <Code code={DrillA_ref} frame="code" lang="txt" title="One correct answer" showLineNumbers={false} class="mt-3" />
          <p class="mt-2">
            <b>If you wrote <code>i ← 0</code>, you skipped a real element and ran off
            the end</b> &mdash; and on the sheet, running off the end stops the program.
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Drill 2: Trace It</h3>
          <p>Exactly what does this display? Every value.</p>
          <Code code={DrillB} frame="code" lang="txt" title="Reference notation" class="mt-3" />
          <p class="mt-3">
            <b>Answer: <code>10 8 6 4</code>.</b> The loop runs <b>until</b> <code>x &lt; 3</code>{" "}
            becomes true &mdash; so it runs while <code>x</code> is still 3 or more.
          </p>
          <p>
            <b class="text-sn-theme">A student who reads <code>REPEAT UNTIL(x &lt; 3)</code>{" "}
            as <code>while (x &lt; 3)</code> gets nothing at all</b> &mdash; <code>10 &lt; 3</code>{" "}
            is false, so their loop never runs. That single inversion is the fourth
            mismatch, and it turns a right answer into a blank.
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Drill 3: The Subtle One</h3>
          <p>What does this display?</p>
          <Code code={DrillC} frame="code" lang="txt" title="Reference notation" class="mt-3" />
          <p class="mt-3">
            <b>Answer: <code>1</code>.</b> On the sheet, <code>bList ← aList</code>{" "}
            makes a <b>copy</b>. Changing <code>bList</code> does not touch{" "}
            <code>aList</code>, so <code>aList[1]</code> is still 1.
          </p>
          <p>
            <b>The same-looking JavaScript gives 99.</b> There <code>let b = a</code>{" "}
            makes both names point at one array, and <code>b[0] = 99</code> changes
            what <code>a</code> sees too. <b class="text-sn-theme">Same code shape,
            opposite answer &mdash; this is the one that inverts your instinct rather
            than shifting an index.</b>
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Drill 4: The Robot</h3>
          <Code code={Robot} frame="code" lang="txt" title="Grid navigation" class="mt-3" />
          <p class="mt-3">
            <b>Answer: facing up, two cells from the start.</b> Right one cell, then{" "}
            <code>ROTATE_LEFT</code> turns it to face up (a left turn from facing right
            is a quarter-turn to up), then forward one more. <b>Two moves, an
            L-shape.</b>
          </p>
          <p>
            <b class="text-sn-theme">The trap is reading <code>ROTATE_LEFT</code> as
            &quot;move left on the grid.&quot;</b> It rotates the robot relative to the
            way it is facing &mdash; the eighth mismatch, and the one nothing else in
            this course teaches.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Apply -->
        <section id="apply">
          <h2>Run the Set Against the Clock</h2>

          <p>
            <b>Barron's and the question bank have dozens of code questions in
            notation.</b> Do a timed set now &mdash; and for every one you miss, name{" "}
            <b>which of the eight</b> caught you.
          </p>

          <ul>
            <li>Set a timer at <b>1 minute 43 seconds per question</b> &mdash; the real pace.</li>
            <li>Miss one? Write the mismatch number next to it, 1 through 8.</li>
            <li><b>A pattern in the numbers is your revision list</b> &mdash; if you keep writing &quot;1&quot;, indexing is the thing to drill, not &quot;notation&quot; in general.</li>
          </ul>

          <p class="mt-3">
            <b>This is the cheapest protection on the exam.</b> Every code question on
            the paper is decided by whether you switched notations correctly, and
            these eight are the whole of the switch.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Checkpoint -->
        <section id="checkpoint">
          <h2>Checkpoint</h2>

          <ul>
            <li>What index is the first element of a list on the reference sheet?</li>
            <li>Does <code>aList ← bList</code> copy the list or share it?</li>
            <li><code>REPEAT UNTIL(c)</code> runs while <code>c</code> is what?</li>
            <li>What happens when a program uses a list index that is out of bounds?</li>
            <li>In the robot block, is a direction relative to the grid or to the robot?</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Submit -->
        <section id="submit">
          <h2>Submit</h2>

          <p>
            Your four drills, and a timed notation set with a mismatch number written
            beside every miss.
          </p>

          <h1
            class="text-center mt-5 text-sn-theme border border-primary slow-flash"
          >
            Activity Complete
          </h1>
        </section>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
